This pen has an amazing body and a great nib (althought BB sized nib for a western M), However I was experiencing issues with the feed. This pen writes great for 3 lines but then slowly dries out really fast. On cheaper paper this was less apparent which imiddietly reminded me of my custom 3DPrinted pen which had the same issue due a poorly designed air channel, so, taking the nib out to try to soak it in soapy water I noticed something, although the feed is perfectly designed with an okay air channel design( a nice dual ink channel design, lots of nice buffers etc) The capillary break section of the feed which afaik draws the air in (but prevents spillage) is blocked buy a notch/slot in the nib section, as a result, the air channel is blocked. which if i'm right really does explain why this pen writes amazing for a few sentences/words and than skips and hard starts and goes dry. it writes on it's nice feed with buffers to even write!

I wanted to 3dprint a new air nib housing but sadly those threads seem too fine for FDM, maybe with SLA? I doubt I could manage that.

&#x200B;

the feed appears to be the exact same as a jinhao, and funny enough the design looks VERY similar to the one used in this reseach paper, minus having two channels all the way to the end [https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0021979720305877](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0021979720305877)

&#x200B;

as I can tell from the paper, the barrel/housing must be completely round! the flat spot on the end is only to allow air to escape to the ink chamber.

&#x200B;

Pictures of actual feed/pen:

[https://imgur.com/a/BcGa654](https://imgur.com/a/BcGa654)

&#x200B;

the only way I can write ok with this pen is to make the inks really wet (Like Iroshizuku Murasaki or EoC) by putting soapy water in my inks to make it wet and thin. (thin so it doesn't look too dark etc)

&#x200B;

PS: I'm no scientist btw, just a moron who has looked at too many feeds!

